  "summary": "The health-tech landscape today reflects significant pressures from multiple directions. Regulatory and political turbulence continues to dominate, with states suing the Trump administration over Medicaid work requirements while a MAHA leader publicly resigned in frustration over what he characterized as industry capture of health policy—creating uncertainty around major coverage and reimbursement changes. On the clinical front, gene therapy safety concerns have emerged after Novartis disclosed seven new tumor cases in children treated with Zolgensma, prompting broader scrutiny of AAV-based therapies despite continued advancement in other areas like J&J's accelerated approval for a bispecific cancer drug. Provider operations face mounting pressures from rising claim denials and increasingly complex payer requirements, while AI adoption in healthcare remains hindered by post-deployment challenges around model drift and inadequate data—issues that could limit returns on significant investments in AI pilots. Several smaller financings and operational updates suggest ongoing activity in wearables and clinical AI tools, though broader questions loom about measurement and sustainability of AI implementations across health systems.",
